/**
  @page examples-shapeoptimization-levelset_cantilever Optimizing a level-set cantilever
  @brief Couple elasticity, descent regularization, transport and body-fitted remeshing.

  The material boundary in this example is represented by the zero set of a
  scalar field. Each iteration solves the elastic body, extends a descent
  direction through its surrounding domain, transports the field and
  reconstructs a fitted mesh. This tutorial explains how those operations
  exchange geometry and fields in `LevelSetCantilever2D`.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-shapeopt-ls-intro Domain representation

  A background domain @f$D@f$ contains material @f$\Omega@f$ and exterior
  cells. Cell labels one and two identify those regions, while facet label
  four identifies their interface. The signed-distance construction assigns
  negative values in the material.
  @f[
    \Omega=\{x:\phi(x)<0\},\qquad\Gamma=\{x:\phi(x)=0\}.
  @f]
  At each iteration, the level set is transported and MMG reconstructs a
  body-fitted interface. Its connectivity and entity numbering can change.

  The objective is compliance plus material-area penalty.
  @f[
    J(\Omega)=\int_\Omega\sigma(u):\varepsilon(u)\,dx+\ell|\Omega|.
  @f]
  The elastic state has a fixed clamp and a downward traction on the loaded
  boundary. The optimizable material interface is traction-free.

  Compliance measures load work, and the positive area penalty assigns a
  cost to retaining material. With the load fixed, the objective balances
  stiffness against material usage. The
  @ref examples-shapeoptimization-simple_cantilever "boundary-deformation tutorial"
  develops the same objective and free-boundary derivative using an explicitly
  displaced material mesh. The examples use different initial domains, area
  weights and update scales; comparisons between their numerical histories
  require matching those choices.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-fields Two meshes and their fields

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Object | C++ storage | Purpose |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | Background @f$D_h@f$ | `th` | Contains both material and exterior cells; carries descent and transport |
  | Material @f$\Omega_h@f$ | `trimmed` | Extracted cell submesh on which elasticity is solved |
  | Elastic displacement @f$u_h@f$ | `u.getSolution()` | Response of the current material body to its load |
  | Design direction @f$\theta_h@f$ | `g.getSolution()`, aliased by `dJ` | Regularized motion extended across the background |
  | Signed distance and transported field | `dist`, `advect.getSolution()` | Encode the current and proposed material interface |
  @endparblock

  Cell and facet attributes belong to different entity dimensions. Cell label
  2 marks exterior material, while facet label 2 marks the clamp. Their equal
  integer values do not identify the same geometric entities.

  @section examples-shapeopt-ls-setup Input and controls

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-setup-includes Modules

  The program combines local elasticity, `Distance::Eikonal`,
  `Advection::Lagrangian`, MMG and XDMF output. These operations each act on
  the mesh and finite element spaces belonging to the current iteration.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-setup-params Parameters

  The Lamé coefficients are @f$\lambda=0.5769@f$ and @f$\mu=0.3846@f$.
  The area penalty is @f$\ell=0.4@f$, the regularization length is
  @f$\alpha=0.1@f$, and the initial MMG size bounds are 0.005 and 0.05.
  A normalized descent field is transported with fixed step 0.0225.
  The default budget is 300 updates.

  The executable takes the hydrated resource
  `LevelSetCantilever2D.mfem.mesh`
  as its first argument. A second positive integer optionally selects the
  iteration budget. Run in a scratch directory; the program creates `out/`
  for its history files.

  Build and run the example from a configured checkout with these commands.

  @code{.sh}
  git lfs pull --include="resources/examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.mfem.mesh"
  cmake --build build --target LevelSetCantilever2D
  mkdir -p results/level-set-cantilever
  cd results/level-set-cantilever
  ../../build/examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D \
    ../../resources/examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.mfem.mesh 300
  @endcode

  Replacing the final argument with a smaller positive integer selects a
  shorter execution check. The 300-state measurements below use the full
  budget. A replacement input mesh must carry the material labels above,
  facet label 4 on the design interface, label 2 on the clamp and label 3
  on the loaded boundary.

  @section examples-shapeopt-ls-loop One iteration

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-loop-remesh 1. Prepare the current geometry

  MMG quality optimization prepares the body-fitted mesh. Facet-to-cell
  incidence is then recomputed for boundary and interface operations.
  Remeshing failures reduce the requested sizes and retry, with a cap of
  eight consecutive failures.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-loop-trim 2. Extract the elastic body

  `th.trim(exterior)` constructs the material submesh. New spaces on that
  submesh describe the state, while full-background spaces describe the
  descent and transport fields.

  This separation gives the state its physical material domain while retaining
  a field in the surrounding cells through which the interface can move.
  The state bilinear form is assembled only on the extracted material body.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-loop-state 3. Solve elasticity

  The state displacement is zero on the clamp and is loaded by
  @f$f=(0,-1)@f$. On the material domain, the weak equation is
  @f[
    \int_{\Omega_h}\sigma(u_h):\varepsilon(v_h)\,dx
      =\int_{\Gamma_N}f\cdot v_h\,ds,
  @f]
  with @f$\varepsilon(u)=\operatorname{sym}\nabla u@f$ and
  @f$\sigma(u)=2\mu\varepsilon(u)+\lambda\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon(u)I@f$.
  Test fields vanish on the clamp. The free interface has the natural
  zero-traction condition.

  @snippet ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp levelset-state

  The conjugate-gradient result is checked before the displacement is used.

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-loop-gradient 4. Extend a regularized descent

  The displacement derivative and interface normal are evaluated from the
  material side. For fixed supports and load boundary, the negative shape
  derivative supplies the density @f$\sigma(u):\varepsilon(u)-\ell@f$.
  The clamped and loaded boundary parts receive zero two-component velocity.

  Write @f$W=\sigma(u_h):\varepsilon(u_h)@f$ and take @f$n@f$ outward
  from the material. The free-boundary derivative density is
  @f$\ell-W@f$, so its negative is used on the right-hand side of
  @f[
    \alpha^2\int_{D_h}\nabla\theta_h:\nabla w_h\,dx
      +\int_{D_h}\theta_h\cdot w_h\,dx
      =\int_{\Gamma_h}(W-\ell)n\cdot w_h\,ds.
  @f]
  The full-background solve spreads the interface load into neighboring
  cells and regularizes its variation. Its coefficient is
  @f$\alpha^2@f$, giving the smoothing length @f$\alpha@f$ in this program.
  The vector @f$\theta_h@f$ then supplies the transport velocity.

  `jac.traceOf(interior)` selects the material-side displacement derivative
  on an internal facet; `n.traceOf(interior)` selects the corresponding
  outward material normal. These choices orient material growth and removal
  consistently. `FaceIntegral` assembles the load on marked internal facets.

  @snippet ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp levelset-gradient

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-loop-advect 5. Construct and transport the distance

  The Eikonal step reconstructs distance from the tagged interface, then
  `sign()` makes the material side negative. The descent is normalized before
  transport. Non-finite norms fail the run; a zero norm ends the updates.

  Distance is reconstructed from the current fitted interface at every
  iteration. Its sign identifies material and exterior, and its approximately
  unit gradient near the interface gives the scalar field a geometric scale.
  The normalized velocity is
  @f$V_h=\theta_h/\max|\theta_h|@f$, using the program's nodal norm sampling.
  The transport problem is
  @f[
    \partial_s\phi+V_h\cdot\nabla\phi=0,
    \qquad \phi(\cdot,0)=d_h,
  @f]
  where @f$d_h@f$ is the signed-distance approximation. The parameter
  @f$s@f$ advances the design; each elastic state is static.
  `Advection::Lagrangian` advances this field along characteristics.

  The fixed increment @f$\Delta s=0.0225@f$ sets a nominal interface-motion
  scale after velocity normalization. It retains its initial value when a
  meshing retry reduces the requested mesh size. Step acceptance therefore
  needs to be assessed together with the current mesh and objective history.

  @snippet ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp levelset-transport

  @subsection examples-shapeopt-ls-loop-recover 6. Recover the interface

  The transported field supplies the zero level set for MMG reconstruction.

  @snippet ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp levelset-discretize

  The next iteration constructs fresh spaces and problems on that new mesh.
  The @ref examples-mmg-behavior "MMG behavior guide" describes preservation
  of required entities and material labels at level-set cuts.

  Both existing cell references are configured to split into material label
  1 and exterior label 2 according to the transported sign. The new interface
  receives facet label 4. MMG can insert, remove and renumber mesh entities
  while constructing this fitted interface. The following iteration extracts
  its material body and recomputes the state, descent and distance fields.

  The output snapshot is written just before this reconstruction. It combines
  the current state with the proposed transported field, so the next frame
  shows the recovered geometry after its preparation step.

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html LevelSetStages-descent.png "Computed normalized descent on the first background mesh. Arrows show direction; the navy contour marks the current material interface."
  @image html LevelSetStages-distance.png "Current signed-distance approximation at the first state. The navy zero contour marks the material boundary; colors saturate outside the interval from -0.05 to 0.05."
  @image html LevelSetStages-transport.png "Transported field after the first design step. Navy marks the current zero contour and cyan the proposed zero contour; the same clipped color interval is used."
  @image html LevelSetStages-remeshed.png "Material mesh used by the second elastic state, after interface reconstruction and preparation. Edges show the fitted triangulation."
  @endparblock

  @section examples-shapeopt-ls-output Inspect the numerical history

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html LevelSetCantilever-initial.png "Elastic state on the initial material mesh."
  @image html LevelSetCantilever-final.png "Elastic state on the last recorded material mesh, using the same color scale."
  @endparblock

  The checked 300-state run reduced the objective from approximately 1.19842
  to 0.942951. Its history contains 138 increasing steps, consistent with a
  fixed-step policy that accepts updates without an objective-decrease test.
  This run establishes successful execution of the specified update sequence.
  Assessing stationarity requires an additional stopping criterion.

  An independent geometry check found positive signed Jacobians in all
  600 exported domain/state grids. Its minimum triangle shape measure was
  approximately @f$8.83\times10^{-7}@f$ at iteration 26. A stage-by-stage
  probe traced this sliver to the preceding level-set reconstruction.
  The minimum was approximately @f$8.39\times10^{-7}@f$ after that cut and
  remained approximately @f$8.83\times10^{-7}@f$ after quality optimization.
  Monitor shape quality as well as Jacobian signs when using this workflow.
  Metric-driven cleanup or step rejection requires its own geometric
  acceptance policy and validation.

  A positive Jacobian records orientation and nonzero cell area. The
  normalized triangle shape measure
  @f[
    q_K=\frac{4\sqrt3\,|K|}
               {\sum_{e\subset\partial K}|e|^2}
  @f]
  equals one for an equilateral triangle and approaches zero for a sliver.
  The recorded small value identifies a cell that remains positive in area
  while having very poor shape. Such cells can affect conditioning and field
  gradients, so geometry checks should accompany an objective plot.

  `obj.txt` records the objective at solved states. The transient XDMF file
  `out/ShapeOptimization.xdmf` contains a full-background **domain** grid with
  descent, distance and transported level set, and a material **state** grid
  with displacement. Open the file in ParaView, select **Apply** and choose
  the grid and field to inspect.

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Output | Interpretation |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `obj.txt` | Weighted objective at each recorded solved state |
  | `domain` block | Background geometry with normalized descent **dJ**, current distance **dist** and transported field **advect** |
  | `state` block | Material submesh with elastic displacement **u** |
  | `out/Omega.<i>.mesh` | Background mesh after the corresponding interface reconstruction |
  @endparblock

  In ParaView, select **Extract Block** to compare `domain` and `state`.
  Inspect the zero contour of **advect** on a current frame, then compare it
  with the next frame's material boundary. On `state`, color by the magnitude
  of **u** and select **Surface With Edges** to inspect the load path and cell
  shapes. The displayed mesh represents the design geometry and the colors
  represent its elastic response.

  The complete run records state indices 0 through 299. The final reconstruction
  is saved as `out/Omega.300.mesh`; its elastic state would require another
  iteration. The observed objective reduction, 138 increasing steps and
  local sliver formation describe separate properties of this run.

  The intermediate-stage figures use recorded states 0 and 1 from the same
  history. `visualize_stages.py --mode levelset`
  renders the exported **dist**, **dJ**, **advect** and next material mesh.

  Fixed-step transport and remeshing define this demonstration. Objective
  histories, interface geometry and solver status should be inspected when
  choosing a step or assessing stationarity.

  @section examples-shapeopt-ls-full Complete source

  Build `LevelSetCantilever2D`. The source
  @ref ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp includes the command-line
  checks, bounded remeshing retries, solve checks and output handling.

  @section examples-shapeopt-ls-see-also Continue

  - @ref examples-shapeoptimization-simple_cantilever "Boundary-variation example"
  - @ref guides-shape-optimization "Derivative and velocity conventions"
  - @ref examples-mmg-behavior "Remeshing behavior"

  @example ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp
  @m_examplenavigation{examples-shapeoptimization-levelset_cantilever,ShapeOptimization/}
  @m_footernavigation
*/
